he buffered reader underneath, so callers holding an HTTPParser can ask 'is there anything coming?' without touching internals."},{"path":"src/httpx/_parsers.py","line":463,"title":"Peek without consuming","kind":"added","now":"Blocks on one read from the socket, immediately puts whatever came back into the pushback buffer so the next real read still sees it, and reports True for data or False for an empty read (which means the peer closed the connection)."},{"path":"src/httpx/_server.py","line":35,"title":"Detect hangup before parsing","kind":"changed","now":"At the top of each loop iteration the server blocks until the client sends bytes; if the peer closed instead, it returns and the handler closes the socket.","prev":"The loop went straight into `_recv_head()`, which read the request line and hit end-of-stream on a closed connection, raising ProtocolError.","impact":"A normal keep-alive hangup is now a silent exit rather than an 'Internal Server Error' entry with a stack trace in the log."},{"path":"src/httpx/_server.py","line":43,"title":"Stale TODO dropped","kind":"removed","prev":"A `# TODO: Handle endpoint exceptions` note sat above the request block.","impact":"None at runtime \u2014 the try/except below it already returns a 500 for endpoint failures, so the note was obsolete."},{"path":"src/httpx/_server.py","line":58,"title":"Comments on drain and reset","kind":"added","now":"Explains that `stream.read()` drains any request body the endpoint never read, and that `_reset()` either returns the parser to idle for the next request or closes the connection."},{"path":"src/httpx/_server.py","line":113,"title":"Ctrl-C no longer swallowed","kind":"changed","now":"`wait()` is a bare loop of one-second sleeps with no exception handling, so a Ctrl-C interrupt propagates out to the caller.","prev":"The sleep was wrapped in a try/except that caught KeyboardInterrupt and broke out of the loop, returning normally.","impact":"Pressing Ctrl-C on a running server now exits with a KeyboardInterrupt traceback instead of a clean return; the server socket is still closed by the surrounding context manager."}],"risks":[{"path":"src/httpx/_server.py","line":113,"text":"Removing the KeyboardInterrupt handler makes `httpx.run(app)` and `scripts/docs serve` terminate with a traceback and a non-zero exit code on Ctrl-C."},{"path":"src/httpx/_server.py","line":35,"text":"wait_ready blocks with no deadline, so an idle keep-alive connection pins one of the five worker threads until the client hangs up \u2014 `_idle_expiry` is still never checked."}],"impacts":[{"symbol":"handle_requests","fromPath":"src/httpx/_server.py","path":"tests/test_client.py","line":25,"kind":"compatible","why":"The fixture's server serves each test then has its connections closed at teardown; those closes now land in wait_ready and exit quietly instead of logging an error."},{"symbol":"handle_requests","fromPath":"src/httpx/_server.py","path":"tests/test_pool.py","line":12,"kind":"compatible","why":"Pooled connections are held open between requests and closed at pool exit, which is exactly the idle-hangup case wait_ready now absorbs; assertions on pool state are unaffected."},{"symbol":"handle_requests","fromPath":"src/httpx/_server.py","path":"tests/test_quickstart.py","line":19,"kind":"compatible","why":"Same server fixture pattern; request handling is unchanged once bytes arrive."},{"symbol":"HTTPServer.wait","fromPath":"src/httpx/_server.py","path":"scripts/docs","line":133,"kind":"behavior-change","why":"The docs dev-server parks in wait(), so stopping it with Ctrl-C now prints a KeyboardInterrupt traceback rather than returning cleanly."},{"symbol":"HTTPServer.wait","fromPath":"src/httpx/_server.py","path":"docs/servers.md","line":37,"kind":"behavior-change","why":"The documented sync example prints 'Press CTRL+C to quit' and calls server.wait(), which no longer quits cleanly on Ctrl-C."}],"impactWindows":{"tests/test_client.py":{"start":5,"lines":["","def echo(req